 "panels": [
  {
   "type": "text",
   "title": "Review: Kubernetes + NVIDIA for GPU workloads",
   "options": {
    "mode": "markdown",
    "content": "\n**Goal.** Show that Kubernetes with NVIDIA's GPU Operator makes GPU work easier to run than the traditional manual way: same model (Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct, LoRA fine-tune, served with vLLM), done twice.\n\n**Machine.** One RTX 4070 (12 GB), native Ubuntu 24.04, driver 590.48.01, k3s, GPU Operator v26.7.2. All numbers below are **single runs on this machine**.\n\n**What we proved**\n- **Self-healing:** a killed training job stays dead on the manual path; on Kubernetes it is training again in about 51 s with nobody involved. A killed server is back and answering in 77 to 81 s.\n- **Sharing:** one GPU, six models. GPU busy 10 to 14% alone, 68% shared. Latency about 2x higher, all six still answer under 0.4 s.\n- **Visibility:** live GPU and pod metrics (this dashboard) come with the stack.\n- **No penalty:** speed and GPU utilization are the same as the manual way.\n\n**What we did not prove:** Kubernetes is not faster, and the first-time setup took about twice as long (images). Growing (more nodes) and Moving (another cloud) were not tested. MIG is covered in theory only.\n"
   },
   "id": 1,
   "gridPos": {
    "x": 0,
    "y": 0,
    "w": 24,
    "h": 9
   }
  },
